+## 6h. The house Talk mode (T8a)
+
+`core/src/home_talk.c` (inside `DOOMO_HAS_TALK`: the native build only until
+TKP), the hooks in `core/src/home_draw.c` (also inside it), the glue types in
+`core/src/home_int.h`, the runner commands in `core/native/runner_main.c`,
+`core/tests/test_home_talk.c` (17 tests), the script
+`core/tests/scripts/home_talk_basic.txt` (320x240 only). Design 4.1-4.3.
+This is **package 1** of T8. The portrait inset, emotes, quick replies,
+catch-the-word, the composer, the typed line and invites are T8b / T8c / T8d
+(see "Where the next packages plug in").
+
+**What you do.** In your own house in Live mode: START, then **Talk** (the row
+before Leave, only when someone is in the room), or walk up to a housemate and
+press **A**. In a visit: START, then Talk (A next to the host still asks what
+they wish for, as before; design 4.1 wants A to open Talk there too: T8d).
+Talk is not offered at 240x160 (a GBA-sized screen; the menu has no row and
+`home_talk_enter()` answers 0).
+
+**What happens.**
+
+1. *Enter.* `home_draw_talk_start()` (reached through the contract's
+   `home_talk_enter(who)`) fills a `HomeTalkRoom`: the room, the free floor, the
+   seat tiles (every `FI_SIT` piece, one per footprint tile), the tables, the door,
+   the player, and everyone in the room (the housemates exactly where Live mode puts
+   them; the host of a visit; the pet in your own house). It calls
+   `home_talk_begin()` and the house goes to `HM_TALK`.
+2. *Who joins and the audience.* **Everyone present joins**, so the room's audience
+   is the whole room: `talk_dm_begin` computes `talk_safe_ctx` over all of them (the
+   adults and kids state is read from the sim by `talk_safe_minor`). The cap is
+   `TALK_PARTS_MAX` (4). When the room holds more, `pick_who()` takes the target
+   first, then children and the pet, then the rest nearest first: **an adult is
+   dropped before a child or the pet**, never the other way, so a child can not be
+   left out of the audience. A child in the room makes the room kid-safe for the
+   whole talk (`any_minor`, `TDF_KIDSAFE`), and nothing here can relax it (it is
+   `talk_dm`'s rule, and the tests check it through the house).
+   `talk_mem_begin` runs after `talk_dm_begin` (what they remember; a romance
+   refusal of today sets `TPF_REFUSED`).
+3. *Gathering* (`gather()`, 12 to 20 frames, longer walks take longer). Residents
+   sit on the **largest cluster of seats** (seats within three tiles of each other),
+   the nearest seat first; the pet never sits. The others stand in a **half circle**
+   round the cluster's middle, or round a table when there is no seat cluster, or
+   round the room's middle: about two tiles out, on the camera's side, not shoulder to
+   shoulder, not on a piece. The player takes a spare seat of the cluster or stands on
+   the open side, a little further out toward the camera. Everyone walks there with the
+   house's walk frames. B leaves even now.
+4. *The camera.* `pick_view()` chooses the quarter turn that shows the seated
+   residents' fronts the most (a seat's rot 0 / 1 shows its front; a tie keeps the
+   view the house is in; with nobody seated the view stays). `home_talk_view()` sets
+   `zoom` 2, `focus_x / y / r` (the group's middle and a tile of head margin), and the
+   pan. The reference renderer draws at one scale and ignores zoom (the T0 contract,
+   `room_ref.c` is **unchanged**; the Dreamcast port relies on it), so the framing is
+   also a **pan**: three quarters of the way from the room's middle to the group's,
+   at most 24 x 8 px, plus the speaker's pan (at most 12 x 6 px). A new speaker
+   boings the `punch_q8` push to 1.1x (`TW_BOING`; native does not scale, the PS1 and
+   Dreamcast will) and pans toward the speaker; an argument beat cuts to a two-shot of
+   the two who row; a sincere line pushes in slowly; quiet for 90 frames goes back to
+   the group shot. **At most one camera move in 45 frames**
+   (`home_talk_cam_stats()`, tested).
+5. *Bubbles.* One at a time, at the speaker's head through `plat_room_project`
+   (the weak default is room_ref's projection; a platform's own wins): 3 lines of 26
+   columns (the lint's rule and `talk_safe_bubble_lines`' wrap), the tail pointing at
+   the head, a name tab, kept on the screen, below the figure when there is no room
+   above, an arrow at the edge for a speaker who is off screen. The typewriter runs at
+   the dialogue's `OPT_TEXT_SPEED` with the speaker's voice babble. A line cut by an
+   interruption ends in "-" and the bubble shakes for 4 frames. The line codes
+   (`{d}`, `{!}` ...) are taken out. At most two bubbles show: the current one and the
+   previous one faded and shrunk to its first line ("..."), gone 150 frames after
+   nobody speaks. The player's own line is a flat-cornered bubble at the bottom left
+   and appears whole; what the gate left of it is shown, so a refusal has no echo.
+   Speakers bob (+-6% at 4 Hz), listeners breathe, an engaged resident faces the
+   speaker, a bored one (`TPF_AWAY`) turns their back, a resident who left
+   (`TPF_LEFT`) walks to the door and is gone once the exchange is on screen.
+6. *Driving the drama manager* (`pump()`): one `talk_dm_step` a frame when no bubble is
+   up (so an exchange can be spread over frames); idle, `talk_dm_tick(1)` (idle chatter
+   after 8 s of nobody talking or a bubble, then the prod); the player's turn
+   (`home_talk_say()`) always goes through `talk_dm_say()` and so through the audience
+   gate: a typed line (`home_talk_parse()` = `talk_parse` with the room as its
+   context), a starter or anything a later package builds. When the talk is over
+   (everyone left, K-7, a goodbye) the last line stays for 40 frames and everyone
+   leaves.
+7. *Leaving.* **B** (or `talk-leave`): the people walk back to where the house had them
+   (12 to 14 frames), `talk_dm_end()` fills a `TalkSummary` (`home_talk_summary()`),
+   the house is Live again and the player stands where the talk put them (sitting, if
+   they took a seat). **Nothing a talk does reaches the sim yet.** T6-OUT has landed (6g):
+   `talk_outcome_begin` is `talk_dm_begin` + `talk_mem_begin` + the refused-today flags
+   (what `home_talk_begin()` does by hand), and `talk_outcome_end` writes the talk memory,
+   calls `talk_dm_end` and applies the summary under the daily caps. T8a was built to use
+   only `talk_dm_end`'s summary (the two packages were landing side by side), so
+   `home_talk_begin()` and `finish()` keep the plain calls; switching them to
+   `talk_outcome_begin` / `talk_outcome_end` is a two-line change for the lander or T8b.
+8. *Control card and instruction line.* The foot of the screen is the shared prompt
+   bar (`ui_prompts_at`): `Pick` (LEFT / RIGHT), `Say it` (A), `Leave` (B); while a
+   bubble is up `Next` (A: finish the line, then go on). One plain instruction line sits
+   above it: "Everyone gathers round. B leaves.", "Choose what to say, then press A.",
+   "They are talking. A skips ahead.", "The chat is over. They head off.", "Bye for
+   now!". Above that, the pick row: LEFT / RIGHT chooses one of five **starters** (Say
+   hi, Ask how they are, Compliment, Tell a joke, Say thanks), a stand-in for T8c's quick
+   replies (the table `starters[]`; each is one act to everyone through the gate).
+
+**The runner** (`core/native/runner_main.c`, the list at its head):
+`family-add ROLE STAGE [NAME]`, `talk-enter [WHO]`, `talk-say TEXT` or
+`talk-say #ACT [SLOT]` (waits for the bubbles on screen, parses, says), `talk-wait`,
+`talk-leave`, `expect-talk on|off`, and `frame-time N [LABEL]` (CPU time per frame, used
+for the numbers below). `core/tests/run_scripts.sh` reads `# resolutions: 320x240` (a
+script that is not for a GBA-sized screen). `home_talk_basic` walks the whole thing:
+START > Talk, the starters, a typed line, B, then A next to a housemate; its shots are
+in the review (Talk is 320x240 only).
+
+**Where the next packages plug in.**
+
+| Package | Where |
+|---|---|
+| T8b portrait inset, emotes (`HVR_*`, "...", music note), the room vibe icon | `home_talk_draw()` (it has the speaker's slot, the line's `face`, and the bubble box in `HtLay`), `home_talk_actors()` (add the squash / z of an emote) |
+| T8c quick replies, catch-the-word, the composer, the typed line | `talk_input()` and the `starters[]` table: each source ends in `home_talk_say()` (the gate is inside `talk_dm_say`); the typed line is `home_talk_parse()` + the DC / native text entry |
+| T8d invites, visit entry (A next to the host), `home_talk_group` / `_safety` / `_visit` scripts, press budgets | `home_draw_talk_room()` (who is in the room; a person arriving through the door joins the room), `home_draw_talk_start()` |
+| T9 pointer | `talk_input()` |
+| TP2 (PS1) | the one static `HomeTalk` (below) moves into the scene scratch; `home_talk_view()` already gives the GTE its framing; `plat_room_project` is the PS1's |
+| the camera on a scaling renderer | `home_talk_view()` (`focus_r`, `punch_q8`) |
+
+**Memory.** Native only, so no console resident byte. Measured on 2026-11-13: the GBA ELF's `.text` 1,403,440 B,
+`.iwram` 11,256 B, `.iwram_bss` 4,084 B, `.data` 816 B, `.bss` 245,788 B (EWRAM) are the same as the origin tip's,
+every symbol and size is the same; the PS1 EXE built with the boot FMV ends at 0x801ef450, **66 KB below the stack
+top** (the same as before: this package adds 0 B; the 72 KB rule is the PS1-RAM package's to meet), and
+`home_draw.c` compiles to the same MIPS code. The state is one static `HomeTalk` in
+`home_talk.c` (1,492 B on x86-64: the `TalkState` 940 B, the summary, two bubbles of
+110 B, the people and the camera), the room it was told about (a `HomeTalkRoom`,
+168 B) and the parser's name buffer (96 B), 1,756 B in all, plus a `HomeScn` field `talk_view` (1 B, native only). TP2 moves them
+into the scene scratch.
+
+**Pixels, proof.** `--frame-hash` of the 100 older runner scripts at 240x160 and 320x240 (200 runs), the
+origin tip against this package: 198 runs identical frame for frame; the two that differ are `home_visit` and
+`home_gallery` at 320x240, and only in the frames where a visit's START menu is open, which now has the row
+**Talk** (before: Give a gift, Photo, Leave). Own house in Live mode with a housemate or the pet in the room gets
+the same row (no older script has one). Nothing changes on 240x160, in Build mode, or in `room_ref`. GBA: the
+ELF's `.text`, `.data`, `.bss`, `.iwram` are the same size, every symbol and its size are the same, and the code
+of `home_draw.o` is the same (`home_talk.c` is an empty unit there).
+
+**Frame time** (native x86-64 host, `frame-time N LABEL`: the CPU time of one frame, update + draw + the
+framebuffer end, at 320x240, the `home_talk_basic` house with two housemates, three runs each on a machine other
+sessions were using; `core/tests/scripts/home_talk_basic.txt`'s setup):
+
+| Frame | origin (4303597) | T8a |
+|---|---|---|
+| Live house, nobody talking | 0.17 - 0.18 ms average, 0.24 - 0.39 ms worst | 0.18 - 0.23 ms average, 0.30 - 0.47 ms worst |
+| Talk, waiting for the player (pick row) | n/a | 0.19 - 0.21 ms average, 0.28 - 0.46 ms worst |
+| Talk, a line typing (bubbles, babble) | n/a | 0.18 - 0.20 ms average, 0.27 - 0.40 ms worst |
+
+So a Talk frame costs the same as a Live frame (the bubble boxes are a few rounded rectangles and a few lines
+of text; the room is the same one `room_ref` draws), about 0.2 ms of the 33.3 ms a 30 fps frame has. That is a
+host number, not a console's: the console cost of Talk is TP2's and TD1's to measure (the PS1's is the GTE room
+draw plus the same 2D). The origin figures need the Live house to be as fast as before: the only change on that
+path is one mode test. `make test`'s scripts hash every other frame the same as origin (below).
+
+**Not built** (and so not tested): the outcomes into the sim (the switch above), the portrait,
+emotes, the room vibe icon, quick replies, the composer, the typed line at the pad,
+invites, pointer input, the table ring with a real table in a premade room (the
+synthetic room test covers it), the camera's scale (native draws at one scale).
+
